title: Bind git_remote_callbacks.push_negotiation so a push can be vetoed from the
  remote's advertised oids (force-with-lease)
updated: 2026-10-07T16:18:34Z
---

`Git::Native::Remote->push` binds `credentials`, `certificate_check`,
`update_tips` and `push_update_reference` of `git_remote_callbacks`, but not
`push_negotiation` (offset 80 in the 1.5.x layout, between
`push_update_reference` at 72 and `transport` at 88):

    int (*git_push_negotiation)(const git_push_update **updates, size_t len, void *payload);
    typedef struct { char *src_refname; char *dst_refname; git_oid src; git_oid dst; } git_push_update;

It runs after the remote advertised its refs and before anything is sent;
`src` is the remote's current target for the ref, `dst` the one about to be
written, and a non-zero return aborts the push. That is the only place a
libgit2 caller can implement what `git push --force-with-lease` does: push a
forced update only if the remote ref still holds the value the caller
expects. libgit2 has no lease option of its own.

Needed by App::karr (its k322): karr's board push is a forced refspec, and two
clones writing in the same pull->write->push window overwrite each other
silently. The fix is a lease per ref against karr's tracking mirror, and with
the native transport there is nothing to hang it on.

Done when:
- `push( ..., negotiation => sub { my (@updates) = @_; ... } )` is called with
  one `{ src_refname, dst_refname, src, dst }` per update (oids as hex, a
  zero oid as undef), and a false return or an exception aborts the push with
  a Git::Native::Error the caller can tell apart from a transport failure;
- the offset is covered the way the other callback offsets are (t/75-style
  layout check where the header is available);
- a test pushes against a local bare remote twice, the second time with the
  remote ref moved in between, and the callback sees the moved `src`.
